You're making the classic error of confusing the meanings of free:

Richard Stallman, the guy who designed the GPL, says this about free
software: "I believe that all generally useful information should be
free. By 'free' I am not referring to price, but rather to the freedom
to copy the information and adapt it to one's own uses.""

http://www.gnu.org/philosophy/free-sw.html expresses this in great
detail. The "freedom" is all about access to the source code, not the
monetary price paid to run the software.

Have some empirical evidence:

You can purchase Redhat Linux. 
You can purchase SuSE Linux. 
You can purchase Novell Linux.
You can purchase Crossover Office. 

